      "eyebrow": "What the check covers",
      "heading": "Routes and reversing",
      "html": "<p>Each point below comes from HSE or the regulations, and I read each one on the official page on 17 November 2026.</p><p><strong>Avoid reversing first.</strong> HSE says to &quot;remove the need for reversing altogether, by setting up one-way systems&quot;. Where reversing cannot be avoided, organise routes to keep it to a minimum. Source: <a href=\"https://www.hse.gov.uk/workplacetransport/factsheets/reversing.htm\">HSE, Safe driving: reversing</a>, checked 17 November 2026.</p><p><strong>Mark the reversing area and keep people out.</strong> HSE says reversing areas &quot;should be planned out and clearly marked&quot;, and people who do not need to be there &quot;should be kept well clear&quot;. Source: <a href=\"https://www.hse.gov.uk/workplacetransport/factsheets/reversing.htm\">HSE, Safe driving: reversing</a>, checked 17 November 2026.</p><p><strong>Site layout.</strong> The Construction (Design and Management) Regulations 2015, regulation 27, says the site must be organised so that, as far as reasonably practicable, pedestrians and vehicles can move without risk. Where possible they are kept apart. There must be effective arrangements to warn anyone at risk of being crushed or trapped that a vehicle is coming. Routes must be regularly checked and maintained. Source: <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2015/51/regulation/27\">legislation.gov.uk, CDM 2015 regulation 27</a>, checked 17 November 2026.</p><p><strong>Mirrors and cameras.</strong> HSE says fixed mirrors &quot;need to be kept clean and in good repair&quot;, and that CCTV can help drivers see behind the vehicle. Source: <a href=\"https://www.hse.gov.uk/workplacetransport/factsheets/reversing.htm\">HSE, Safe driving: reversing</a>, checked 17 November 2026.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "What the check covers",
      "heading": "The banksman, signals and lifting",
      "html": "<p><strong>Banksmen are a last resort.</strong> HSE: &quot;Banksmen should only be used in circumstances where other control measures are not possible.&quot; They can still be used for reversing, but only if trained and authorised. Source: <a href=\"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/faq-banksmen.htm\">HSE, Banksmen and signallers</a>, checked 17 November 2026.</p><p><strong>The driver stops if the banksman goes out of sight.</strong> HSE says that if drivers lose sight of the signaller &quot;they should know to stop immediately&quot;. The banksman must be visible to the driver at all times. Source: <a href=\"https://www.hse.gov.uk/workplacetransport/factsheets/reversing.htm\">HSE, Safe driving: reversing</a>, checked 17 November 2026.</p><p><strong>Agree standard signals before work starts.</strong> HSE says to agree on the use of standard signals. They are in the Health and Safety (Safety Signs and Signals) Regulations 1996, Schedule 1, Part VIII (verbal communication) and Part IX (hand signals). Sources: <a href=\"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/faq-banksmen.htm\">HSE, Banksmen and signallers</a> and <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1996/341/contents\">legislation.gov.uk, SI 1996/341</a>, checked 17 November 2026.</p><p><strong>A safe position and distinctive hi-vis.</strong> On the same HSE page, the wording on protected positions and distinctive high-visibility clothing is written for signallers, who direct crane drivers during lifting. It is sensible practice for a banksman too, but to be straight with you, HSE's page does not say it in those words for banksmen. Source: <a href=\"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/faq-banksmen.htm\">HSE, Banksmen and signallers</a>, checked 17 November 2026.</p><p><strong>Lifting.</strong> If plant is lifting, regulation 8 of the Lifting Operations and Lifting Equipment Regulations 1998 (LOLER) says every lifting operation must be properly planned by a competent person, appropriately supervised and carried out in a safe manner. Source: <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1998/2307/regulation/8\">legislation.gov.uk, LOLER regulation 8</a>, checked 17 November 2026.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The checklist",
      "heading": "Twelve items, Yes, No or N/A",
      "html": "<p>Do it before plant moves for the day, and again if the layout changes.</p><div class=\"checklist-box\"><ul><li>1. Can reversing be avoided today? Is a one-way or drive-through route in use where possible?</li><li>2. Are the reversing areas marked so drivers and pedestrians can both see them?</li><li>3. Are people who do not need to be in the reversing areas kept out?</li><li>4. Are pedestrian routes and crossing points separated from vehicle routes, and clear?</li><li>5. Is a banksman needed today because other controls are not possible? If yes, is the banksman named?</li><li>6. Is the banksman trained and authorised by the employer, and is that recorded?</li><li>7. Have the driver and banksman agreed the standard signals and who is in charge of the move?</li><li>8. Does the driver know to stop at once if the banksman goes out of sight?</li><li>9. Does the banksman have a safe standing position, visible to the driver and out of the vehicle's path, in distinctive hi-vis? (Good practice, see above.)</li><li>10. Are mirrors clean and in good repair, and is any camera screen working?</li><li>11. Are warning devices on the plant working (alarm, and beacon or lights where fitted)?</li><li>12. Have delivery and visiting drivers been told the site rules before they come in?</li></ul></div><p>Item 12 follows HSE's advice that visiting drivers should know your site rules (<a href=\"https://www.hse.gov.uk/workplacetransport/information/safedrivers.htm\">HSE, Choosing and training safe drivers</a>, checked 17 November 2026). HSE's reversing page says audible alarms should be loud and distinct, but I could not confirm that HSE asks for a daily check of beacons or lights. Treat item 11 as your own site rule.</p><p>If any answer is No, stop the movement. Fix it or change the plan, and note who fixed it and when.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Competence",
      "heading": "A certificate is not the whole answer",
      "html": "<p>HSE says banksmen may be used only if trained and authorised (<a href=\"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/faq-banksmen.htm\">HSE, Banksmen and signallers</a>, checked 17 November 2026). Under the Provision and Use of Work Equipment Regulations 1998 (PUWER), regulation 9, every employer must make sure that people who use work equipment have had adequate training, and so has anyone who supervises or manages its use (<a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1998/2306/regulation/9\">legislation.gov.uk, PUWER regulation 9</a>, checked 17 November 2026).</p><p>A course certificate shows training happened. Authorisation is the employer's call. So for each banksman, record:</p><ul><li>their name, and the date the employer authorised them</li><li>who assessed them and how (watched on site, not just a certificate)</li><li>the training evidence you hold, and when it was done</li><li>a review date</li></ul><p>Quickler does not provide banksman training. If you need a course, look for an approved training provider in your area. I do not name or recommend one.</p>"
    },
