      "eyebrow": "Sources",
      "heading": "Sources and review date",
      "html": "<p>Last reviewed 17 November 2026. I opened and read every source below on that date.</p><ul><li>HSE, <a href=\"https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/faq-banksmen.htm\">Banksmen and signallers</a></li><li>HSE, <a href=\"https://www.hse.gov.uk/workplacetransport/factsheets/reversing.htm\">Safe driving: reversing</a></li><li>HSE, <a href=\"https://www.hse.gov.uk/workplacetransport/information/safedrivers.htm\">Choosing and training safe drivers</a></li><li>HSE, <a href=\"https://www.hse.gov.uk/workplacetransport/checklist/section5.htm\">Workplace transport checklist, section 5, vehicle movements</a></li><li>legislation.gov.uk, <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2015/51/regulation/27\">CDM 2015, regulation 27, traffic routes</a></li><li>legislation.gov.uk, <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1998/2306/regulation/9\">PUWER 1998, regulation 9, training</a></li><li>legislation.gov.uk, <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1998/2307/regulation/8\">LOLER 1998, regulation 8, organisation of lifting operations</a></li><li>legislation.gov.uk, <a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1996/341/contents\">Health and Safety (Safety Signs and Signals) Regulations 1996</a></li></ul><p>This is a practical checklist, not legal advice. It does not replace your own risk assessment or your site's traffic management plan. For the wider site walk, see the <a href=\"/pages/h-and-s-site-walkround-checklist-uk.html\">H&amp;S site walkround checklist</a>.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "faq",
      "heading": "Questions, answered",
      "html": "<h3>How often should a banksman and plant movement check be done?</h3><p>HSE does not set a number for this particular check. Most sites do it daily, before plant moves, and again when the layout changes. Do it often enough that a changed route or a blocked walkway is caught before someone gets hurt.</p><h3>Do I always need a banksman?</h3><p>No. HSE puts removing the need to reverse first, using one-way systems, and says banksmen should only be used where other control measures are not possible. If you do use one, they must be trained and authorised.</p><h3>Does a banksman training certificate make someone competent?</h3><p>A certificate shows training. HSE says banksmen must be trained and authorised, and PUWER regulation 9 puts the duty on the employer to make sure people have adequate training. Record the employer's own assessment and authorisation as well.</p><h3>What if the driver cannot see the banksman?</h3><p>The driver stops at once. HSE says drivers should know to stop immediately if they lose sight of the banksman or signaller.</p><h3>Does Quickler provide banksman training?</h3><p>No. Quickler records the daily check and writes it up. Banksman training comes from an approved training provider.</p><h3>Is this a legal checklist?</h3><p>It is a practical checklist built on HSE guidance and the regulations named above. It does not replace your own risk assessment or your site's traffic management plan.</p>"
    }
